    "q-80466": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80466",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{y}(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2}).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2y(y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
