    "q-132894": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132894",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)-{g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})-{g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
